# (28,5) is the tile the Gym door drops you on, and a text box is open when you
# land: instrumenting the live env there showed `wFontLoaded=1` on every worker,
# every step. A forced movement rule cannot clear a text box, and `force_action`
# *replaces* the policy's own choice -- so the policy picked b 173 times in one
# episode and executed it 0 times, while Right was issued 988 times against a
# dialogue that never closed. 96/96 workers pinned, 979 consecutive steps.
#
# So the route above now starts at (28,6) and this tile is shaping-only,
# rewarding B (closes the box) and Right (the direction the route wants). Third
# time this exact shape has bitten: the Fuchsia nurse tile and the Koga tile were
# both the same "one forced action cannot both dismiss and move" problem. Rule
# of thumb: never let a forced movement leg own a tile you arrive on by warp or
# finish a conversation on.
# A healed worker leaves the Center on (28,19). Pre-Koga that tile belonged to
# the row-28 Gym leg, but that leg is Koga-gated now, so post-Koga it owns
# nothing and the worker is stranded halfway along row 28. Walk it back west to
# (28,6), where FUCHSIA_TO_SAFARI_GATE_STEPS starts.
# 2026-08-09: the direct Safari run, and the reason the old one looped.
#
# FUCHSIA_TO_SAFARI_GATE_STEPS goes south to row 32, west to x=1, then north --
# which is the Fuchsia ENTRY route's corridor walked backwards. The entry route
# is unscoped and listed first, so it won its tiles: (30,8) and (29,8) forced Up
# against the Safari route's Down, (32,8) Up against Left, (32,1) Right against
# Up. Workers ran a closed loop -- (28,6) down, (30,6) right, (30,8) UP back to
# (28,8), left to (28,6), repeat -- which is exactly the blob the swarm sat in:
# 3,072 episodes, all `progressless_stall`, all `340/402 -> 402/402` (they healed
# every episode and then looped, never leaving town).
#
# This route leaves from the frontier tile and goes east along row 20 and up the
# east side instead. 80 steps rather than 104, it shares no tile with the entry
# route, and it never touches row 28 -- so the HP-gated Center approach never
# fires and the heal detour disappears with it. That is fine here: the Safari
# Zone has no trainer battles and you cannot faint in it, so arriving at 85% HP
# costs nothing.
